  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Elena and Eleonora explain that because work is medically forbidden for Viktor Petrovich, Anton was hired and brought his own cooks, firing Barinov's team. Outraged, Chef Barinov challenges the newcomers to a culinary duel for their jobs.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, тебе совсем нельзя нервничать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Тяжёлая работа тебе сейчас противопоказана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Он привёл сюда свою команду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Ты будешь вести общее руководство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он всех моих поваров уволил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда убирайте моё авторское меню!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И меняйте вообще название ресторана!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он уволил лучших поваров Москвы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они готовят намного лучше вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай поставим моих против твоих.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "А мне нравится эта идея.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Давайте устроим сегодня честный поединок.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, тебе совсем нельзя нервничать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, you must not get nervous at all.' The grammar packs 'to you' and 'to be nervous' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Тяжёлая работа тебе сейчас противопоказана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Heavy work is forbidden for you right now.' Notice how Russian expresses 'heavy' and 'contraindicated' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Он привёл сюда свою команду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He brought his own team here.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'he' before landing on 'team'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Ты будешь вести общее руководство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will handle general supervision.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'management' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он всех моих поваров уволил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did he fire all of my cooks?' Notice how Russian expresses 'he' and 'to fire' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда убирайте моё авторское меню!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then take away my signature menu!' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'then' and 'clear' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И меняйте вообще название ресторана!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And change the name of the restaurant altogether!' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'restaurant' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он уволил лучших поваров Москвы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He fired the best cooks in Moscow.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'he' and finishing on 'Moscow'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они готовят намного лучше вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They cook much better than you.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'they', while 'you' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай поставим моих против твоих.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us put my guys against yours.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'your'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "А мне нравится эта идея.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I actually like this idea.' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'idea' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Давайте устроим сегодня честный поединок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us hold a fair duel today.' Eleonora anchors the line on 'let's', while 'duel' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
